#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637682 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(M)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2-(-12) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637686 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =7\\cdot 8-0\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =56-0 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637691 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(X)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(X) & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639182 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=v{e}^{2u},\\,y={e}^{u}+w,\\,z=2u+w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2v{e}^{2u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{2u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2v{e}^{2u} & {e}^{2u} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2v{e}^{2u} & {e}^{2u} \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2v{e}^{2u} & {e}^{2u} \\\\ {e}^{u} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-2{e}^{2u})+(0-{e}^{u+2u}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2u}-{e}^{3u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639317 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3v & -7 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-16$, what is the value of $v?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3v & -7 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3v)\\cdot (-3)-(-7)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =9v+56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-16$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-16 \\\\ 9v+56 & =-16 \\\\ 9v & =-16-56 \\\\ 9v & =-72 \\\\ v & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639318 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-6-8r & 2-6r \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(U)=182$, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6-8r & 2-6r \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-6-8r)\\cdot 7-(2-6r)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-42-56r-10+30r \\\\  & =-52-26r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=182,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =182 \\\\ -52-26r & =182 \\\\ -26r & =234 \\\\ r & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639325 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(C)=16,$ determine the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-4)-k\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =8+8k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(C)=16$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =16 \\\\ 8+8k & =16 \\\\ 8k & =16-8 \\\\ 8k & =8 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639327 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ -4k & 8k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-6$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ -4k & 8k+2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (8k+2)-(-6)\\cdot (-4k) \\\\  & =-48k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-6$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-6 \\\\ -48k-6 & =-6 \\\\ -48k & =-6-(-6) \\\\ -48k & =0 \\\\ k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639331 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 7k & 7k-5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-89$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 7k & 7k-5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (7k-5)-7\\cdot 7k \\\\  & =7k-40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-89$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-89 \\\\ 7k-40 & =-89 \\\\ 7k & =-89-(-40) \\\\ 7k & =-49 \\\\ k & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639340 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$ given that\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -6 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7} & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n$det(D)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -6 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7} & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 7-(-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{7}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{12}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639341 :question/problem "Calculate $det(E)$ given that\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n$det(E)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(E) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-5)-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639345 :question/problem "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-4x & -4y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 8y\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4x & -4y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 8y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4x\\cdot 8y-(-4y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-32xy+2xy \\\\  & =-30xy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639354 :question/problem "Calculate $det(H)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2t & 7u \\\\ -2r & 7s\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(H) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2t & 7u \\\\ -2r & 7s\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2t\\cdot 7s-7u\\cdot (-2r) \\\\  & =-14st-(-14ru) \\\\  & =14\\left(ru-st\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639363 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -2 \\\\ y & xy\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}6x & -2 \\\\ y & xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6x\\cdot xy-(-2\\cdot y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y-(-2y) \\\\  & =2y\\left(3{x}^{2}+1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639371 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5+p & -2p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -4-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=38,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5+p & -2p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -4-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-5+p)(-4-p)-(-2p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =20+5p-4p-{p}^{2}+2p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =20+3p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =20+3p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=38$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =38 \\\\ 20+3p & =38 \\\\ 3p & =18 \\\\ p & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639375 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7+p & -6p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=23,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7+p & -6p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(7+p)(2-p)-(-6p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14-7p+2p-{p}^{2}+6p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =14+p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =14+p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=23$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =23 \\\\ 14+p & =23 \\\\ p & =9 \\\\ p & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639377 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4+p & 7p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -5-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=-180,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4+p & 7p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -5-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4+p)(-5-p)-(7p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-20-4p-5p-{p}^{2}-7p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-20-16p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =-20-16p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-180$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =-180 \\\\ -20-16p & =-180 \\\\ -16p & =-160 \\\\ p & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639378 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3+p & 8p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=82,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3+p & 8p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3+p)(4-p)-(8p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12-3p+4p-{p}^{2}-8p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =12-7p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =12-7p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=82$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =82 \\\\ 12-7p & =82 \\\\ -7p & =70 \\\\ p & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639425 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a422cb07a0eed88f8c13aa25f0a776ea9ad4b445ab5cffc678f8b224c2ea7d7a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{15 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2},\\qquad y=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{3} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}\\big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=15 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\qquad y'(t)=- 9 \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{- 9 \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{15 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3} \\tan{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{5}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}&=\\frac{- 9 \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}}{15 \\cos{\\left(2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}}\\\\&=\\frac{- \\frac{27}{2}}{\\frac{15}{2}}\\\\&=- \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639426 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f453eda11cc0af8ff36a826e896aae15465317a31cff1e2c3d4ba6598635abe.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=15 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\qquad y=16 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{8}$. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=30 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\qquad y'(t)=- 32 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{- 32 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{30 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}=- \\frac{16 \\tan{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{15}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{8}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\big|_{t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{8}}&=\\frac{- 32 \\sin{\\left(2 \\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{8}\\right) \\right)}}{30 \\cos{\\left(2 \\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{8}\\right) \\right)}}\\\\&=\\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{15 \\sqrt{2}}\\\\&=\\frac{16}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639432 :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1ec0b7a502b5955006203395593b7cb23eef468f8651d49182fc59095ac5395.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{33 t^{2}}{2} - 11 t,\\qquad y=6 - 6 t^{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=3$. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=33 t - 11,\\qquad y'(t)=- 12 t\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{- 12 t}{33 t - 11}=- \\frac{12 t}{33 t - 11}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=3$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\big|_{t=3}&=\\frac{- 12 \\left(3\\right)}{33 \\left(3\\right) - 11}\\\\&=\\frac{-36}{88}\\\\&=- \\frac{9}{22}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639439 :question/problem "![](images/99/99b118406eb0c67ce3c2ca8508d2223402f2d78537f09b9dad20a4844c090474.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2 \\sqrt{3} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\qquad y=- \\frac{35 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}\\big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- 4 \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\qquad y'(t)=- 35 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{- 35 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{- 4 \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}=\\frac{35 \\sqrt{3}}{12 \\tan{\\left(2 t \\right)}}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}&=\\frac{- 35 \\cos{\\left(2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}}{- 4 \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}}\\\\&=\\frac{- \\frac{35}{2}}{-6}\\\\&=\\frac{35}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639442 :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b1c9f6aa0dd5690ef3d2b83f235c5676b4e59ec728a23641632460d4b0f0a08.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=7 t e^{t},\\qquad y=t^{2} + 2 t\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1$. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=7 t e^{t} + 7 e^{t},\\qquad y'(t)=2 t + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2 t + 2}{7 t e^{t} + 7 e^{t}}=\\frac{2 e^{- t}}{7}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\big|_{t=1}&=\\frac{2 \\left(1\\right) + 2}{7 \\left(1\\right) e^{\\left(1\\right)} + 7 e^{\\left(1\\right)}}\\\\&=\\frac{4}{14 e}\\\\&=\\frac{2}{7 e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640208 :question/problem "![](images/61/614dc0ba892aa7f2d74b32e97aa3363f521353c8ab631f809d2d54fee9127cb4.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos {t},\\,y=6 \\sin {2 t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- 2 \\sin {t},\\,y'(t)=12 \\cos {2 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(12 \\cos {2 t}\\right)}{\\left(- 2 \\sin {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{6 \\cos {2 t}}{\\sin {t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{6},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} & =- \\frac{6 \\cos {2 \\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}\\right)}}{\\sin {\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{6 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640213 :question/problem "![](images/6e/6ea91c863313b2a9fca67b8f4b2e38666d0fddbedbb974b396372777a7843e42.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\cos {t},\\,y=3 \\sin {3 t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- \\sin {t},\\,y'(t)=9 \\cos {3 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(9 \\cos {3 t}\\right)}{\\left(- \\sin {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{9 \\cos {3 t}}{\\sin {t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}} & =- \\frac{9 \\cos {3 \\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)}}{\\sin {\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{9 \\left(0\\right)}{\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640214 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d367f75c6b66cb0e215770f4eb36f281487f470cb420e4fb7666422b80269f54.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2 \\sin {t},\\,y=2 \\cos {t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2 \\cos {t},\\,y'(t)=- 2 \\sin {t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(- 2 \\sin {t}\\right)}{\\left(2 \\cos {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\tan {t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}} & =- \\tan {\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640218 :question/problem "![](images/a2/a275e758044f59c6b6659d82749eeca15a9e120515d5a3b693957914ae3d42a2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4 \\sin {t},\\,y=2 \\cos {t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4 \\cos {t},\\,y'(t)=- 2 \\sin {t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(- 2 \\sin {t}\\right)}{\\left(4 \\cos {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\tan {t}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi}{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}} & =- \\frac{\\tan {\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640223 :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6 \\cos {t},\\,y=2 \\sin {3 t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- 6 \\sin {t},\\,y'(t)=6 \\cos {3 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(6 \\cos {3 t}\\right)}{\\left(- 6 \\sin {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\cos {3 t}}{\\sin {t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =- \\frac{\\cos {3 \\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)}}{\\sin {\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\left(0\\right)}{\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640738 :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b511e6494fc9f5ec653321f29ae2628c48222ef42f591f4ce39a36c60948481.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{11}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{121}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{121}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641752 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta\\,(\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta\\,(\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta\\cdot 1}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\cancel{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta}\\,\\sin \\theta}{\\cancel{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta}\\,\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=6\\,\\tan \\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta} \\\\ &=6\\,\\tan \\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641765 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta+\\sin^{4} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta+\\sin^{4} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta\\,(\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta\\,(\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta\\cdot 1} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\cancel{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}\\,\\sin \\theta}{\\cancel{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}\\,\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=6\\,\\tan \\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos^{4} \\theta+\\sin^{4} \\theta\\,\\cos^{2} \\theta} \\\\ &=6\\,\\tan \\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641767 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{4} t\\,\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{4} t}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{4} t\\,\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{4} t} \\\\ &=\\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{2} t\\,(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{2} t\\,(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)} \\\\ &=\\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{2} t\\cdot 1} \\\\ &=\\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{2} t} \\\\ &=\\frac{2\\,\\cancel{\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}\\,\\sin t}{\\cancel{\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}} \\\\ &=\\frac{2\\,\\sin t}{1} \\\\ &=2\\,\\sin t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2\\,\\sin^{3} t\\,\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{4} t\\,\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t\\,\\cos^{4} t} \\\\ &=2\\,\\sin t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641777 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{4} \\phi+\\sin^{3} \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{4} \\phi+\\sin^{3} \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi} \\\\ &=\\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi\\,(\\sin^{2} \\phi+\\cos^{2} \\phi)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi\\,(\\sin^{2} \\phi+\\cos^{2} \\phi)} \\\\ &=\\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi\\cdot 1} \\\\ &=\\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi} \\\\ &=\\frac{3\\,\\cancel{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi}\\,\\cos \\phi}{\\cancel{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi}} \\\\ &=\\frac{3\\,\\cos \\phi}{1} \\\\ &=3\\,\\cos \\phi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{3\\,\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{3} \\phi}{\\sin \\phi\\,\\cos^{4} \\phi+\\sin^{3} \\phi\\,\\cos^{2} \\phi} \\\\ &=3\\,\\cos \\phi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186641778 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta\\,(\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta\\,(\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta\\cdot 1}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\cancel{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}\\,\\sin \\theta}{\\cancel{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}} \\\\ &=\\frac{6\\,\\sin \\theta}{1} \\\\ &=6\\,\\sin \\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{6\\,\\sin^{3} \\theta\\,\\cos^{3} \\theta+6\\,\\sin^{5} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta}{\\sin^{2} \\theta\\,\\cos \\theta} \\\\ &=6\\,\\sin \\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642351 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,x)=⟨x,\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.222em}8\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,x)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64}=1.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64}=1$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,x) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642427 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $1+\\frac{(1)\\cdot (\\sin^{2} \\alpha)}{2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha}.$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions. Then we expand the numerator, group the squared terms, and use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{(2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha)+\\sin^{2} \\alpha}{2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha}{2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{2+(\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha)}{2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{(2+1)}{2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{3}{2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used $\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha=1$. Therefore, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{3}{2+\\cos^{2} \\alpha}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642430 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $2\\cdot \\cot \\theta-\\frac{4-2\\cdot \\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the quotient ratio as a fraction. Then put both terms over a common denominator and combine the signed numerators.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{2\\cdot \\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}-\\frac{4-2\\cdot \\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta-4\\cdot \\sin \\theta+2\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\theta}{\\cos \\theta\\cdot \\sin \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta)-4\\cdot \\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta\\cdot \\sin \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{2-4\\cdot \\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta\\cdot \\sin \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used $\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta=1$. The simplified expression is $\\frac{2-4\\cdot \\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta\\cdot \\sin \\theta}$, on the domain of the original expression."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642443 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos \\alpha(\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand the outer factor and combine the fractions. Cancel the shared factor, retaining the exclusions of the original denominators.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{\\cos \\alpha(4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha+3\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha)}{\\cos \\alpha\\cdot \\sin \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\alpha+3\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{4\\cdot (\\sin^{2} \\alpha+\\cos^{2} \\alpha)-\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha} \\\\ & =\\frac{4-\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used $\\cos^{2} \\alpha+\\sin^{2} \\alpha=1$. The simplified expression is $\\frac{4-\\cos^{2} \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}$, on the domain of the original expression."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642454 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $3\\cdot \\tan \\theta+\\frac{4+3\\cdot \\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the quotient ratio as a fraction. Then put both terms over a common denominator and combine the signed numerators.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{3\\cdot \\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}+\\frac{4+3\\cdot \\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\sin^{2} \\theta+4\\cdot \\cos \\theta+3\\cdot \\cos^{2} \\theta}{\\sin \\theta\\cdot \\cos \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{3\\cdot (\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)+4\\cdot \\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta\\cdot \\cos \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{3+4\\cdot \\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta\\cdot \\cos \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used $\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta=1$. The simplified expression is $\\frac{3+4\\cdot \\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta\\cdot \\cos \\theta}$, on the domain of the original expression."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642459 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{-2\\cdot \\sin^{2} t}{1-\\cos t}+3\\cdot \\cos t.$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator and combine the fractions. Expand the numerator, apply the Pythagorean identity, and group the common factor before cancelling.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{-2\\cdot \\sin^{2} t+3\\cdot (\\cos t(1-\\cos t))}{1-\\cos t} \\\\ & =\\frac{-2\\cdot \\sin^{2} t+3\\cdot \\cos t-3\\cdot \\cos^{2} t}{1-\\cos t} \\\\ & =\\frac{-2+3\\cdot \\cos t-\\cos^{2} t}{1-\\cos t} \\\\ & =\\frac{-2\\cdot (1-\\cos t)+(\\cos t(1-\\cos t))}{1-\\cos t} \\\\ & =-2+\\cos t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used $\\cos^{2} t+\\sin^{2} t=1$. The simplified expression is $-2+\\cos t$, on the domain of the original expression."}]}
